nd T1-2-3 \u2014 not helpful.\n\nNo.\n\nAnother idea: **Knitted Straight** (12 points)? Requires 1,4,7 of one suit; 2,5,8 of second; 3,6,9 of third. I have B1, B8, B9 \u2014 close to 1,8,9 \u2014 not matching.\n\nT1,T2,T3,T5,T6,T7 \u2014 scattered.\n\nPerhaps **Greater/Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles**? But I have almost no knitted structure.\n\nBack to basics: I need to discard safely and preserve potential.\n\nBest preserved value: **T1-T2-T3** and **T5-T6-T7** as two chows in same suit (Bamboos). That gives me two chows. Already have one meld. Need two more chows and a pair.\n\nPair: B1\u00d72 is good. Keep it.\n\nNow, among discards, safest to break is **W8** \u2014 isolated, not part of any sequence, not terminal (W8 is 8, not 1/9), and opponent has W5-W6-W7, so W8 might be slightly dangerous to them? But W9 has been played multiple times, so maybe they are not holding 789.\n\nAlso, W7 is down to 2 unshown \u2014 possibly one in wall, one in hand. W8 has 3 unshown \u2014 relatively safe.\n\nMoreover, **W8 is not useful for any major pattern in my hand** \u2014 unlike the Bamboo sequences.\n\nDiscarding **T3, T1, T2, T5, T6, T7** would break potential chows.\n\nDiscarding **B1** breaks the only pair.\n\nDiscarding **B9** or **B8** loses terminal tile \u2014 but B8 and B9 are part of possible B7-B8-B9 chow \u2014 but I have B5-B6-B7 already, so B7 is used. Cannot reuse B7.\n\nSo B8 and B9 are lone terminals \u2014 not helping any new chow unless I had B6-B7, but B7 is already melded.\n\nThus, B8 and B9 cannot form chow with anything in hand. Only way is if I draw B7 again \u2014 but B7 has 3 unshown, possible, but unlikely to help.\n\nSimilarly, B1\u00d72 is the only pair.\n\nSo **B1 pair should be kept**.\n\nThus, best discard candidates: W8, B8, B9, or one of the T singles \u2014 but avoid breaking T1-T2-T3 or T5-T6-T7.\n\nWait \u2014 I have **only one of each T tile**, so T1,T2,T3 can form one chow; T5,T6,T7 another. If I discard any, I lose one chow.\n\nBut I