used one in meld (exposed)\\n- I hold one in hand\\n- So 2 used \\u2192 2 remain \\u2192 unshown should be 2\\n\\nBut given: T6: 3 unshown \\u2192 impossible.\\n\\nLikely error in interpretation.\\n\\nPerhaps \\u201cunshown\\u201d means **not revealed on table**, i.e., excluding discards and exposed melds, but including all hidden tiles (in hands and wall).\\n\\nBut even so, total is 4.\\n\\nLet\\u2019s define:\\n- \\\"Unshown\\\" = number of tiles of that type that are neither in any exposed meld nor discarded.\\n\\nSo for T6:\\n- Exposed in my meld: 1\\n- In my hand: 1 \\u2192 this is \\\"hidden\\\", not shown to others\\n- So total revealed (to table): only the one in meld\\n- Discards: check if T6 was discarded.\\n\\nMy history: Play T6? No. I played T9, B9, etc.\\n\\nPrevious player: Play T6 \\u2192 yes! One T6 discarded.\\n\\nSo:\\n- One T6 in my meld (exposed)\\n- One T6 discarded by previous player\\n- One T6 in my hand\\n\\u2192 3 accounted for \\u2192 one left unshown \\u2192 but given T6: 3 unshown \\u2192 contradiction.\\n\\nGiven: T6: 3 unshown \\u2192 implies only one T6 has been revealed (in discard or meld)\\n\\nBut:\\n- My meld [T5,T6,T7] reveals one T6\\n- Previous player discarded one T6\\n\\u2192 two T6 revealed \\u2192 so unshown should be 2\\n\\nBut given as 3 \\u2192 impossible.\\n\\nUnless the \\\"unshown\\\" list is incorrect or misinterpreted.\\n\\nPossibility: \\u201cunshown\\u201d means **remaining in the wall only**, not including opponents\\u2019 hands.\\n\\nBut that would be unusual.\\n\\nAlternatively, typo or data issue.\\n\\nBut let's proceed pragmatically.\\n\\nDespite data ambiguity, core logic remains.\\n\\nI have:\\n- Hand: T1, T2, T3, T6, T7, B1, B1, B8\\n- Melds: [T5,T6,T7], [B5,B6,B7]\\n\\nSo I have two exposed chows.\\n\\nPotential third chow: [T1,T2,T3] \\u2014 pure chow.\\n\\nThen I need a fourth meld and a pair.\\n\\nI have B1\\u00d72 \\u2014 can be pair.\\n\\nRemaining tile: B8 \\u2014 singleton.\\n\\nSo if I keep [T1,T2,T3], B1\\u00d72, and B8, I have three chows, one pair, one singleton \\u2014 need to convert B8 into a meld.\\n\\nBut B8 cannot form a chow without B6/B7/B9 \\u2014 B6 and B7 are already used in my meld [B5,B6,B7], and B9 has been discarded multiple times (by me and others), so B9 may be rare.\\n\\nB8 could pair with itself \\u2014 but I only have one.\\n\\nSo B8 is useless unless I draw B8 or B7/B9.\\n\\nBut B7: unshown 1 \\u2192 exists\\nB8: unshown 2 \\u2192 exist\\nB9: unshown 1 \\u2192 exists\\n\\nBut forming [B7,B8,B9] is possible, but risky.\\n\\nAlternatively, discard B8 and keep the structure around [T1,T2,T3] + pair B1.\\n\\nNow, what about T6 and T7 in hand?\\n\\nT6: I have one in hand, but T6 was used in meld \\u2014 is that allowed? Yes, since 4 copies.\\n\\nBut do I have a use for extra T6?\\n\\nCould I form [T6,T6,T6]? But I only have one in hand, one in meld \\u2014 total two.\\n\\nNot enough.\\n\\nT7: unshown 0 \\u2192 all four T7 are out.\\n\\nWhere are they?\\n- One in my meld\\n- One in my hand\\n- One discarded? Not seen\\n- Perhaps someone has a meld?\\n\\nAcross player has [W5,W6,W7], not T.\\n\\nNext player has [T8,T8,T8]\\n\\nNo sign of other T7.\\n\\nBut unshown T7: 0 \\u2192 must all be out.\\n\\nSo likely: two T7 in my possession (one in meld, one in hand), and two discarded or in others\\u2019 hands.\\n\\nAssume it\\u2019s correct.\\n\\nSo I hold T7 \\u2014 but no use for it beyond the existing chow.\\n\\nNow, my viable waits:\\n\\nIf I go for hand: [T1,T2,T3], [T5,T6,T7], [B5,B6,B7], and a fourth meld, plus pair.\\n\\nFourth meld must come from either:\\n- Another chow: e.g., involving B8 \\u2192 needs B7/B9\\n- Pung: e.g., B1\\u00d73, but I only have two\\n- Or keep B1 pair and make another chow\\n\\nBest chance: aim for [T1,T2,T3] as chow, keep B1 pair, and hope to draw B1 or B8 to complete something.\\n\\nBut better: consider discarding a tile to improve shape.\\n\\nCurrent hand after removing meld tiles: effectively, I control:\\n- Singletons: T1, T2, T3, T6, T7, B8\\n- Pair: B1\\u00d72\\n\\nBut I must discard one.\\n\\nTarget: complete a legal hand.\\n\\nPossible winning patterns:\\n\\n- All Chows (2 points): if I win with four chows and a pair of suited tiles \\u2192 valid, since B1 is suited.\\n\\nCurrently have two chows, can get third with [T1,T2,T3], need fourth chow.\\n\\nFourth chow possibilities:\\n- Use T6 and T7? Need T5 or T8 \\u2192 T5: unshown 3 \\u2192 possible, but T5 is mid-game.\\n- [T6,T7,T8]? I have T6, T7 \\u2192 need T8\\n- But T8: next player has a pung [T8,T8,T8] \\u2192 likely no more T8 (only 4 copies)\\n\\u2192 T8 probably exhausted \\u2192 cannot form [T6,T7,T8]\\n\\nSimilarly, [B7,B8,B9]: I have B8, B7 is in my meld (used), so only one B7 free? No \\u2014 I used one B7 in [B5,B6,B7], but there are 4 copies.\\n\\nUnshown B7: 1 \\u2192 exists\\n\\nB9: unshown 1 \\u2192 exists\\n\\nSo possible to draw B7 or B9 to form [B7,B8,B9]\\n\\nBut I only have one B8 \\u2014 so I\\u2019d need to draw B7 or B9 to complete the chow, then discard T-something.\\n\\nAlternatively, discard B8 now \\u2014 it\\u2019s the least connected tile.\\n\\nCompare options:\\n\\nDiscard candidates:\\n- T1: part of potential chow [T1,T2,T3]\\n- T2: same\\n- T3: same\\n- T6: isolated, no easy extension\\n- T7: same, and T7 is fully used up (all 4 out), so no one can use mine, but I can\\u2019t form chow without T8\\n- B1: I have a pair \\u2014 breaking pair risks losing pair\\n- B8: standalone, hard to use\\n\\nBest discard: T6 or T7 or B8\\n\\nT6: could be used in [T4,T5,T6] or [T5,T6,T7] but [T5,T6,T7] already exists in my meld, and others may avoid it.\\n\\nBut across player has [T2,T3,T4] \\u2014 so they had T2,T3,T4 \\u2014 possibly collecting terminals/middle.\\n\\nBut no sign they want T5/T6.\\n\\nStill, T6 is somewhat central.\\n\\nT7: all four out \\u2192 very hot tile \\u2014 dangerous to discard.\\n\\nIndeed, if unshown T7: 0, then anyone needing T7 to win could self-draw or claim mine.\\n\\nSo discarding T7 is extremely dangerous \\u2014 likely to feed a win.\\n\\nSimilarly, T8: already Peng'd \\u2014 very dangerous.\\n\\nT7 is similar.\\n\\nSo **do not discard T7**.\\n\\nT6: less dangerous, but still possible wait.\\n\\nB8: unshown 2 \\u2192 two left \\u2192 safer.\\n\\nB8 can be used in [B7,B8,B9] or [B6,B7,B8] or [B8,B8,B8]\\n\\nBut B6: unshown 1, B7: 1, B9:1 \\u2192 all low frequency.\\n\\nBut no one has shown interest.\\n\\nAlso, I have B1 pair \\u2014 valuable as pair.\\n\\n[T1,T2,T3] is a strong chow \\u2014 pure, simple.\\n\\nSo best strategy: keep [T1,T2,T3], B1 pair, and try to draw a tile to form fourth chow.\\n\\nFourth chow options:\\n- Draw B2 \\u2192 [B1,B2,B3]? But I have no B3\\n- Draw B3 \\u2192 same\\n- Draw B7 or B9 to pair with B8\\n\\nBut B8 is weak.\\n\\nAlternatively, discard B8 now, and wait to draw a tile that completes a chow with T6 or something.\\n\\nBut T6-T7-x needs T5 or T8 \\u2014 T5 possible, T8 unlikely.\\n\\nBetter to discard a tile that is least useful.\\n\\nT6 is in short supply (unshown 3 \\u2192 actually high? Wait: unshown T6: 3 \\u2192 many left) \\u2014 but I have one, and one discarded? Earlier conflict.\\n\\nAssume data is correct: unshown T6: 3 \\u2192 so only one revealed \\u2192 likely the one in my meld \\u2192 so three left \\u2192 safe.\\n\\nBut I hold one \\u2192 so two others in play.\\n\\nStill, T6 is not terminal.\\n\\nBut compared to B8, T6 is more connectable.\\n\\nT1,T2,T3 are needed for chow.\\n\\nB1\\u00d72 is pair.\\n\\nSo the weakest tile is **B8**.\\n\\nIt has no connection to any existing set.\\n\\nWhereas T6 could potentially form [T6,T6,T6] if I draw more, but unlikely.\\n\\nOr [T5,T6,T7] \\u2014 but I already have that meld, and drawing another T5/T7 won't help.\\n\\nSo T6 is dead weight too.\\n\\nBut T6 could be part of a pair wait or something.\\n\\nHowever, the only viable path is to keep [T1,T2,T3] as a chow, keep B1 pair, and hope to draw a tile that forms a fourth chow.\\n\\nThe most flexible way is to reduce redundancy.\\n\\nNote: I have two sources of T6 and T7:\\n- One T6 and one T7 in meld\\n- One T6 and one T7 in hand\\n\\nSo I could treat the hand T6 and T7 as a potential [T6,T7,x] chow if I draw T5 or T8.\\n\\nT5: unshown 3 \\u2192 good availability.\\n\\nT8: unshown 0 \\u2192 all four out \\u2192 cannot draw T8.\\n\\nSo only possible extension: [T5,T6,T7] \\u2014 but I already have that meld, and it\\u2019s exposed.\\n\\nBut I could form another copy? No, melds are sets.\\n\\nI cannot have two [T5,T6,T7].\\n\\nSo no.\\n\\nThus, extra T6 and T7 are useless unless forming pungs.\\n\\nBut I don\\u2019t have three.\\n\\nSo both T6 and T7 in hand are essentially dead tiles.\\n\\nSame for B8.\\n\\nBut I must keep three tiles to complete hand: ideally, the [T1,T2,T3] chow.\\n\\nSo among T6, T7, B8 \\u2014 all bad \\u2014 but I must discard one.\\n\\nWhich is safest?\\n\\n- T7: unshown 0 \\u2192 all four are out \\u2192 if someone is waiting for T7 to complete a chow like [T5,T6,T7] or [T7,T8,T9], they might win off my discard.\\n\\nGiven that across player has [T2,T3,T4] and [W5,W6,W7], not T, and next player has T8s, possible someone is going for upper straights.\\n\\nBut no evidence.\\n\\nStill, T7 is a common middle tile.\\n\\nB8: less commonly used.\\n\\nAlso, B8 is not involved in any major pattern.\\n\\nMoreover, I have no other B-tiles except B1 and B5-B7 (used).\\n\\nSo B8 is isolated.\\n\\nTherefore, **discard B8**.\\n\\nIt minimizes damage to my potential [T1,T2,T3] chow and keeps flexibility.\\n\\nFinal decision:\\n\\nAnswer: Play B8\\n\\nReason: B8 is a disconnected singleton with little potential to form a meld, while T1,T2,T3 can form a chow, and keeping T6/T7 offers slight chance of pairing or being useful in a rare case. Discarding B8 preserves the structure of a potential All Chows hand.\\n\\nEvaluation:\\n- Keeping [T1,T2,T3] supports Mixed Straight or Mixed Shifted Chows\\n- B1 pair is usable\\n- Avoid discarding T7 due to risk (all 4 out)\\n- B8 is safest discard\\n\\nSituation: I have an exposed hand with two chows, holding fragments for a third chow [T1,T2,T3], a pair B1, and loose tiles T6, T7, B8. With T7 being a high-risk discard (all copies out), and B8 having no combinational value, it is optimal to discard B8 to work towards a tenpai hand centered on [T1,T2,T3] and future draws.\\n\\nEvaluation: \\n- Play B8: removes a dead tile, keeps promising [T1,T2,T3] and pair B1\\n- Play T6 or T7: risks breaking future options or feeding wins\\n- Other plays destroy the chow potential\\n\\nReason: B8 is the least connected tile with no path to a meld, while preserving [T1,T2,T3] maximizes chances to complete a legal hand with All Chows or Mixed Straight patterns.\\n\\nAnswer: Play B8\"}"}
