7: already all four used? Unshown T7:0 \\u2192 yes, all out:\\n- One in my meld [T5,T6,T7]\\n- One in opposite\\u2019s meld [T2,T3,T4]? No \\u2014 that\\u2019s T2,T3,T4\\n- My meld uses one T7\\n- Previous player played T7? No\\n- I played T7? In history: no\\n- But T7 unshown:0 \\u2192 all four accounted for.\\n\\nWhere are they?\\n1. My chow [T5,T6,T7] \\u2014 one\\n2. Opposite player\\u2019s [T2,T3,T4] \\u2014 no T7\\n3. Next player: played T3, T1, W7, W3, B2, J2, B4, B7, Peng T8, B3, W1, B8, W4, B7, T3, T1 \\u2014 no T7\\n4. Previous player: played B7, J3, J1, W2, B5, B9, F3, J3, B8, B5, B6, B3, T6 \\u2014 no T7\\n5. Me: played T9, B9, W8, T8, W9, B9, F1, W8, B8, T8, W9, B9, F1, T8, B8 \\u2014 ah! I played T8, but not T7\\n\\nWait \\u2014 where are the four T7s?\\n\\nUnshown: T7:0 \\u2192 so all four are either in hands or melds.\\n\\nOnly meld known to use T7 is mine: [T5,T6,T7]\\n\\nOthers:\\n- Opposite: [T2,T3,T4] \\u2014 no\\n- Next: [T8,T8,T8] \\u2014 no\\n- Previous: none\\n\\nSo three T7s unaccounted.\\n\\nPossibly in hands.\\n\\nBut I might still hold one? But I formed chow using one T7.\\n\\nUnless I had two.\\n\\nSuppose I had T5, T6, T7, T7 \\u2192 used one T7 in chow, kept one.\\n\\nThen unshown: 4 - 1 (mine) - 1 (in chow) = 2? No: total 4 copies.\\n\\nUsed in my meld: one T7.\\n\\nRemaining: 3.\\n\\nIf I hold one, then two elsewhere.\\n\\nBut unshown count is 0 \\u2192 means no T7s left in wall or undiscarded? No: \\\"unshown\\\" means not seen in discards or melds \\u2014 so if in hand, still unshown.\\n\\n\\\"Unshown tiles are\\\" list gives counts still potentially in play (in hands or wall).\\n\\nT7:0 \\u2192 so no T7s remain unseen \\u2192 all four have been revealed via melds or discards.\\n\\nBut only one T7 is in my meld.\\n\\nOthers must have been discarded.\\n\\nCheck discard histories:\\n\\n- Me: no T7 played\\n- Next: no T7\\n- Opposite: no T7\\n- Previous: no T7\\n\\nBut I played T8 multiple times, not T7.\\n\\nContradiction.\\n\\nUnless the system considers \\\"played\\\" only recent, but history seems complete.\\n\\nAlternatively, error in data.\\n\\nBut accept T7:0 \\u2192 so T7 is completely used up \\u2192 I cannot hold T7.\\n\\nTherefore, **T7 cannot be in my hand**.\\n\\nSimilarly, valid action \\\"Play T7\\\" must be invalid \\u2014 but it's listed.\\n\\nUnless I have it.\\n\\nOnly explanation: I used one T7 in meld, and still hold another \\u2014 but then at least two T7s are in my possession.\\n\\nAnd other two must be discarded.\\n\\nBut no one played T7.\\n\\nUnless I did.\\n\\nIn my history: Play T9, Play B9, Play W8, Play T8, ... \\u2014 I played T8, but not T7.\\n\\nClosest: Play T8.\\n\\nPerhaps typo.\\n\\nOr perhaps \\\"T7\\\" in hand is a mistake.\\n\\nGiven the conflict, safest to assume that **T7 is not safe to discard because it might not exist**, but since it's in valid actions, maybe I do have it.\\n\\nAlternative: perhaps the chow [T5,T6,T7] was formed using a discarded T7, not T6.\\n\\nBut history says \\\"Chi T6\\\" \\u2192 which means I completed the chow [T5,T6,T7] by claiming a discarded T6.\\n\\nSo I held T5 and T7, claimed T6.\\n\\nThen T7 was in my hand, now part of meld.\\n\\nSo after forming meld, T7 is no longer in hand.\\n\\nThus, I cannot have T7 now.\\n\\nTherefore, **valid action \\\"Play T7\\\" should not be possible** \\u2014 contradiction.\\n\\nOnly resolution: the hand list is inaccurate, or \\\"T7\\\" refers to something else.\\n\\nPerhaps it's a duplicate entry.\\n\\nGiven the confusion, focus on viable discards that make sense.\\n\\nAmong T1, T2, T3, T6, T7 \\u2014 T7 is impossible (all gone), so likely not.\\n\\nT6: unshown 3 \\u2192 possible.\\n\\nBut let's discard risky tiles.\\n\\nT1: \\n- I played T9, not T1\\n- Next player played T1 twice\\n- Opposite played T1\\n- So T1 heavily discarded \\u2192 relatively safe\\n\\nT2: \\n- I have T2\\n- Opposite has [T2,T3,T4] meld \\u2192 already has three T2s? No, chow uses one T2\\n- They could want T2 for pair or kong\\n- But they discarded T4, T3, T1 \\u2014 no T2\\n- So possibly safe\\n\\nT3:\\n- Similar \\u2014 opposite has [T2,T3,T4] \\u2192 one T3 used\\n- They played T3 \\u2014 so likely safe\\n\\nT6:\\n- Previous player played T6\\n- I might have extra\\n- Not much info\\n\\nBest move: discard a tile that completes no obvious wait.\\n\\nNote that I have T1,T2,T3 in hand \\u2014 could form [T1,T2,T3] chow if I keep them.\\n\\nCurrently I have no chow in lower T.\\n\\nIf I can form [T1,T2,T3], then I'd have:\\n- [B1,B1,B1]\\n- [B5,B6,B7]\\n- [T5,T6,T7]\\n- [T1,T2,T3]\\n\\nThat's four melds, all chows.\\n\\nThen need a pair.\\n\\nAny tile could become pair if I draw duplicate.\\n\\nPatterns:\\n- **All Chows (2 points)**\\n- **No Honors (1 point)**\\n- **One Voided Suit (1 point)** \\u2192 only T and B, no W\\n- Total: 4 points \\u2014 insufficient to win (need 8)\\n\\nNeed more.\\n\\nIs there a chance for **Lower Tiles (24 pts)**? Only if all tiles are 1,2,3 \\u2014 but I have B5,B6,B7 and T5,T6,T7 \\u2014 no.\\n\\n**Middle Tiles**? B5,B6,B7 and T5,T6,T7 fit, but B1,B1,B1 does not \\u2014 B1 is not middle.\\n\\n**Upper/Lower** no.\\n\\n**Triple Pung**? Only one pung.\\n\\n**Two Dragon Pungs**? None.\\n\\nSo limited.\\n\\nBut wait \\u2014 **Three Kongs**? No.\\n\\nBack to discarding.\\n\\nIf I keep T1,T2,T3, I can aim for [T1,T2,T3] chow.\\n\\nTo do that, I need to discard either T6 or T7.\\n\\nT7 is likely unavailable, so probably discard T6.\\n\\nT6 is less useful: I already have a 5-6-7 in both suits.\\n\\nAnother option: keep T6 and aim for [T4,T5,T6] or [T6,T7,T8], but T8 is konged, T7 gone.\\n\\nSo [T4,T5,T6] possible if I draw T4.\\n\\nBut T4 unshown:2 \\u2014 possible.\\n\\nBut weaker than securing lower end.\\n\\nMoreover, opposite player has [T2,T3,T4] \\u2014 so if I try to chi T4, they might discard it, but also they could be waiting for it.\\n\\nBut they already have a complete chow \\u2014 so T4 might be safe.\\n\\nBut safer to go for [T1,T2,T3].\\n\\nDiscard T6.\\n\\nThen, if I draw T4, I can chi for [T2,T3,T4] or [T4,T5,T6], but [T1,T2,T3] is better because T1,T2,T3 are all in hand.\\n\\nWith T1,T2,T3 in hand, I can chi any of T1,T2,T3 if discarded.\\n\\nFor example, if someone discards T1, I can chi to form [T1,T2,T3] using my T2,T3.\\n\\nSimilarly for T2 or T3.\\n\\nSo keeping T1,T2,T3 gives me three chances to complete a chow.\\n\\nWhereas keeping T6 gives only one path.\\n\\nAlso, T6 is more commonly available (unshown 3), so less likely to be picked.\\n\\nTherefore, best to discard T6 to protect the T1-T2-T3 sequence.\\n\\nReason: Maximizes opportunity to form a fourth chow while building toward a safer, more flexible hand. The T1-T2-T3 sequence offers multiple chi opportunities, and discarding the less strategic T6 reduces clutter.\\n\\nAnswer: Play T6\"}"}
